Subject: Re: possible pkgsrc emacs framework improvements?
To: Thomas Klausner <wiz@NetBSD.org>

(DRAFT -- not sent.)

Hi Thomas,

The rest of the sweep finished, so here are the numbers I owed you.
Of the 67 packages that select emacs30nox, 40 build, 2 I did not try,
and 25 fail -- ten because of this change and fifteen for reasons that
have nothing to do with it.

The ten fall into three shapes, and each takes one line to fix.

Seven have a PLIST that follows ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX} while the install
writes the path itself, so the file check catches the mismatch:
gnuserv, matlab-mode, twittering-mode, dictem, emacs-dict-client,
xslide and emacs-w3m.  Two of these are worth a word.  editors/gnuserv
lists share/doc/${PKGBASE} in INSTALLATION_DIRS while installing to
share/doc/gnuserv, so README ends up as a file of that name rather
than inside a directory; and editors/matlab-mode creates its directory
as ${PKGBASE:C|^xemacs-||} but installs into ${PKGBASE}, which means
it has been broken under xemacs all along and nobody noticed because
the two spellings agreed under emacs.  I fixed both and they build.

Two get a 404 fetching their distfile, because mk/fetch/github.mk has
GITHUB_PROJECT?= ${PKGBASE} and the prefix moves PKGBASE, so the fetch
asks for github.com/ziglang/emacs30nox-zig-mode: emacs-neotree and
markdown-mode.  I fixed zig-mode and lua-mode the same way earlier and
both build, so the one line is enough.

One names a renamed package without the prefix and can then never be
satisfied: misc/emacs-wiki asks for semi>=1.14.6.  devel/flim was the
same, with an unprefixed apel>=10.8 next to an apel buildlink3.mk that
already says ${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}apel>=10; I fixed that one and it
builds.

Two of the things that had to be fixed were in my own modules.mk, and
only building found them.  EMACSLOADPATH for the build still named the
old shared site-lisp, so anything byte-compiling against another elisp
package stopped with "Cannot open load file" -- devel/flim could not
require apel's install.el.  Nothing in the checks I had showed this:
EMACS_LISPPREFIX was right, and the installed Emacs finds install.elc
with the same flags the build uses.  Only the environment variable
pointed at the old place.  Separately, the EMACS_VERSION_REQD path
still had the empty-_EMACS_PKGDIR fatal I thought I had fixed, because
I had only fixed it on the EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED side.

A third was in something I had already told you was done.  I wrote
that the choice reads the package's list in the order it was written,
the way pyversion.mk walks PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED, and that was the
intent but not the code: the loop that intersects the two lists walked
modules.mk's, so the package's order never reached the answer.
misc/mailcrypt writes emacs20 first and still got emacs31.  The loop
runs the other way now, and moving mailcrypt's line moves what it
picks, which is how I checked.

The pbulk part was worse, and I am sorry to have said it was done.  I
wrote that it was three lines in mk/pbulk/pbulk-index.mk of the same
shape as the python and ruby entries.  It was three lines and they did
nothing: the class also has to be named in _PBULK_MULTI, which is the
list the loop walks, so nothing ever read them.  It also wanted the
filtered list rather than the written one, for the reason python takes
_PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED -- otherwise a package naming a version
pkgsrc no longer has puts it in the bulk index.  So it is four lines,
and with them devel/apel gives

  PKGNAME=emacs30nox-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs29-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs29nox-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs30-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs31-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs31nox-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2

with the default first, while devel/pcl-cvs, which takes emacs20 and
nothing else, gives the single line it should, and devel/gmake, which
never includes modules.mk, is untouched.

Nine of the fifteen unrelated failures never reached the elisp package
at all.  All nine stopped in the same place, www/curl, several levels
down -- under ruby34, cmake, meson or luatex -- and that failure was
mine rather than the tree's: I had two sweeps of my own running
against one WRKOBJDIR for a while, and curl was left with a
half-configured work directory.  Cleaning it out and building again
gave curl 8.21.0.  So those nine say nothing either way and I have not
counted them for or against the change.  Two of them I am building
again now that curl is there; the other seven sit behind rust, texlive
or a gcc bootstrap, which I would rather not ask this machine for.

Three failures are distfiles that no longer fetch (cobol-mode,
flyspell, ispell-emacs), one is a dependency marked broken on x86_64
(rsltc), one is a ruby package that does not build here
(migemo-elisp), and two I did not try at all because they build bazel
first and that has nothing to do with where the lisp goes
(mozc-elisp, mozc-elisp333).

One failure is the change refusing, which is what you asked for.
mail/vm in my copy depends on misc/bbdb2, which accepts only emacs21,
and the build stops with

  ERROR: No Emacs that pkgsrc still has is accepted here
  ERROR: Accepted versions are: emacs21 emacs21nox
  ERROR: pkgsrc has: emacs31 emacs31nox ... xemacs214nox

rather than with "Cannot open /version.mk".  I have to say plainly
that this cannot happen on trunk: you took bbdb2 out with emacs21, and
mail/vm rev 1.47 no longer includes its buildlink3.mk.  The same goes
for something else I wrote last time -- I said fifteen packages still
ask for emacs21, and that is true of my 2 September copy and not of
pkgsrc, since jde, leim21, bbdb2 and nxml-mode went with the version.
Nothing on trunk names it.

So the refusal is worth showing with EMACS_VERSION_REQD instead, where
the two reasons stay apart:

  devel/apel, EMACS_VERSION_REQD=emacs21
    "pkgsrc no longer has emacs21"
    "pkgsrc has: emacs31 emacs31nox ... xemacs214nox"

  devel/apel, EMACS_VERSION_REQD=emacs20
    "This package does not build with emacs20"
    "Accepted versions are: emacs29 emacs29nox ... emacs31nox"

The intersection is worth taking for the next retirement rather than
for a mess sitting there today.

Since my earlier figures came from that copy, I took them again from
trunk, fetching each Makefile and PLIST.  The build results above are
still from the copy, with the current modules.mk dropped in; every
package I name still exists on trunk with the same lines.  87 packages
read modules.mk, leaving out wip and the emacs and xemacs packages
themselves.  Of the 86 with a PLIST, 77 already write
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX} and six write the path by hand -- the same six I
named before.  Of the 26 that pass a lisp directory into their build,
25 pass the variable; only chat/zenicb does not.

Three of those six need a second line, because the install writes the
shared path as well and changing only the PLIST splits the two.  I
found two of them by building -- inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi and
devel/cqual, both of which leave it to a bundled makefile -- and the
third, devel/rsltc, by reading: its own Makefile has
${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/share/emacs/site-lisp/rsl spelled out three times.
I have not built rsltc; it wants devel/gentle, which is marked broken
on x86_64.

The name is the expensive half, and most of it is outside modules.mk.
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX only does anything where the package spells it in
PKGNAME, and 22 of the 87 do not.  Adding it moves PKGBASE, and six
variables under mk/ take ${PKGBASE} as their default -- PKGWILDCARD
and PKG_SYSCONFVAR in bsd.pkg.mk, FILEBASE in
pkgformat/pkg/package.mk, and GITHUB_PROJECT, GITLAB_PROJECT and
CODEBERG_PROJECT in mk/fetch -- so anything resting on the default
moves with it.  Ten of the 87 fetch from GitHub without setting
GITHUB_PROJECT; none uses gitlab or codeberg.  Nine spell ${PKGBASE}
themselves, and since PKGBASE is in PLIST_SUBST a package that uses it
on both sides stays consistent while one that uses it on a single side
does not.  misc/color-theme has it in MASTER_SITES, so its Savannah
fetch would move too.

One last thing, which is not about placement at all.
EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE is set by devel/apel, and no line of
modules.mk reads it, while pyversion.mk and rubyversion.mk both
subtract their equivalent from what they will choose from.  Now that
the selection walks the package's own list, leaving it out is one more
condition in the same loop, so I have done that.  mail/mu picks
emacs31 here and emacs31nox when given EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE=
emacs31.  apel's own line names emacs20, which it does not accept
anyway, so nothing moves there -- that line has been doing nothing
twice over.

Happy to send the framework part on its own first, with the package
fixes following in whatever grouping suits you.

Showta
